Git Basics

Q: What is Git and how is it different from GitHub?
Git is a distributed version control system that tracks code changes locally. GitHub is a cloud platform that hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration features (PRs, issues, actions). Example: you can use Git fully offline on your laptop; you only need GitHub when you want to push/share that code with a team.

Q: What's the difference between centralized and distributed version control?
Centralized (e.g., SVN) has one central server — if it goes down, no one can commit. Distributed (Git) gives every developer a full copy of the repo history locally. Example: if your office internet goes down, you can still commit, branch, and view history locally — you just can't push until it's back.

Q: How do you initialize a Git repository?

git init

Example: you created a new project folder my-app/ and want to start tracking it — run git init inside it, and it creates a hidden .git folder.

Q: How do you clone an existing repository?

git clone https://github.com/user/repo.git

Example: joining a new team, you clone their repo to get the full codebase and history on day one.

Configuration

Q: How do you set your username/email for commits?

git config --global user.name "Priya Sharma"
git config --global user.email "priya@example.com"

Example: first time setting up Git on a new laptop — without this, commits fail or use a wrong identity.

Q: How do you check your current Git config?

git config --list
Staging and Committing

Q: What are the three states of a file in Git?
Modified (changed, not staged) → Staged (marked for next commit) → Committed (saved in repo history).

Q: How do you check the status of your working directory?

git status

Example: before committing, you always run this to see which files are modified, staged, or untracked.

Q: How do you stage files?

git add filename.js      # single file
git add .                # all changed files
git add -p               # interactively pick chunks to stage

Example: you fixed a bug and also left some debug console.logs in the same file — git add -p lets you stage only the bug-fix lines, not the debug lines.

Q: How do you commit staged changes?

git commit -m "Fix login validation bug"

Example: a good commit message describes what and why: "Fix null pointer on empty cart checkout" — not just "fix bug".

Q: How do you amend the last commit?

git commit --amend -m "New message"

Example: you just committed but forgot to add one file or made a typo in the message — instead of a new messy commit, amend it (only safe if not yet pushed/shared).

Viewing History

Q: How do you view commit history?

git log
git log --oneline --graph --all

Example: --oneline --graph gives a compact visual of branches merging — useful to explain a messy history in a standup.

Q: How do you see what changed in a specific commit?

git show <commit-hash>

Q: How do you see differences between working directory and last commit?

git diff              # unstaged changes
git diff --staged     # staged changes vs last commit

Example: before running git add, you run git diff to double-check you're not accidentally committing a hardcoded API key.

Branching

Q: Why do we use branches?
To work on features/fixes in isolation without affecting the stable main branch. Example: you create feature/payment-gateway so your half-finished work doesn't break main for the rest of the team.

Q: Common branch commands?

git branch                     # list branches
git branch feature/login       # create branch
git checkout feature/login     # switch to it
git checkout -b feature/login  # create + switch in one step
git branch -d feature/login    # delete branch (safe)
git branch -D feature/login    # force delete

Q: git checkout vs git switch?
checkout is the older, multi-purpose command (branches, files, commits). switch is newer and specifically for changing branches, added to reduce confusion. Example: git switch main is clearer intent than git checkout main, though both work.

Merging and Rebasing

Q: How do you merge a branch into main?

git checkout main
git merge feature/login

Example: your feature is tested and approved — you merge it into main so it's part of the next release.

Q: git merge vs git rebase?
Merge combines two branch histories with a new merge commit (preserves full history, non-linear). Rebase replays your branch's commits on top of another branch's latest commit (linear, cleaner history but rewrites commit hashes). Example: before opening a PR, you git rebase main to bring in the latest changes and avoid a messy merge commit; but you'd never rebase a branch that teammates have already pulled, since it rewrites history they depend on.

Q: What's a fast-forward merge?
When the target branch hasn't diverged at all, Git just moves the pointer forward instead of creating a merge commit. Example: you branched off main, made 2 commits, nobody else touched main — merging back is a simple fast-forward.

Q: How do you resolve a merge conflict?

Git marks conflicted files with <<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>>
Manually edit to keep the correct code
git add <file>
git commit (or git rebase --continue if rebasing)

Example: you and a teammate both edited line 20 of config.js — Git can't auto-merge, shows both versions, you pick (or combine) the right one and mark it resolved.

Q: How do you abort a merge or rebase mid-conflict?

git merge --abort
git rebase --abort

Example: conflicts are messier than expected and you want to start over cleanly — abort resets you to the state before you started.

Undoing Changes

Q: How do you discard local changes to a file?

git checkout -- filename.js
# or, newer syntax:
git restore filename.js

Example: you were experimenting with a config change that broke things — restore it back to the last committed version.

Q: How do you unstage a file (keep changes, remove from staging)?

git restore --staged filename.js

Q: git reset — soft, mixed, hard?

--soft: undo commit, keep changes staged
--mixed (default): undo commit, keep changes unstaged
--hard: undo commit AND discard changes completely

Example: you committed too early — git reset --soft HEAD~1 lets you edit and recommit; git reset --hard HEAD~1 is for when you want to throw that commit away entirely (dangerous, use carefully).

Q: git revert vs git reset?
Revert creates a new commit that undoes a previous one (safe for shared/pushed history). Reset rewrites history (only safe for local/unpushed commits). Example: a bad commit was already pushed and deployed — you git revert <hash> instead of resetting, so the history stays intact for the team.

Q: How do you recover a deleted branch or lost commit?

git reflog
git checkout <hash-from-reflog>

Example: you accidentally ran git reset --hard and lost commits — git reflog shows recent HEAD movements so you can recover the "lost" commit hash.

Stashing

Q: What is git stash used for?
Temporarily saves uncommitted changes so you can switch branches cleanly, then reapply them later.

git stash              # save changes
git stash list         # view stashes
git stash pop          # reapply and remove from stash
git stash apply        # reapply but keep in stash list

Example: you're mid-way through a feature when an urgent bug comes in — you git stash, switch to main, fix the bug, then come back and git stash pop to resume exactly where you left off.

Remote Repositories

Q: How do you connect a local repo to a remote?

git remote add origin https://github.com/user/repo.git

Q: git push vs git pull vs git fetch?

push: send local commits to remote
fetch: download remote changes but don't merge them into your branch
pull: fetch + merge in one step (git pull = git fetch + git merge)

Example: before starting work each morning, git fetch lets you see what changed on main without touching your current branch; git pull actually brings those changes in.

Q: How do you push a new local branch to remote for the first time?

git push -u origin feature/login

The -u sets upstream tracking so future git push/git pull on that branch don't need the branch name repeated.

.gitignore

Q: What is .gitignore used for?
Tells Git which files/folders to never track. Example: a .gitignore with node_modules/, .env, *.log — so dependency folders and secrets never get committed.

Q: A file is already tracked but you want to ignore it now — what do you do?

git rm --cached filename

Then add it to .gitignore. Example: someone accidentally committed a .env file with credentials — adding it to .gitignore alone won't remove it from tracking; you need git rm --cached .env too.

Tags

Q: What are Git tags used for?
Marking specific commits as release points. Example: git tag v1.0.0 marks the exact commit that went to production for v1.0, so you can always check out that exact state later.

git tag v1.0.0
git push origin v1.0.0
Common Scenario Questions

Q: You committed to the wrong branch — how do you fix it?

git checkout correct-branch
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
git checkout wrong-branch
git reset --hard HEAD~1

Example: you meant to work on feature/A but committed on main by mistake — cherry-pick moves that exact commit to the right branch, then you remove it from main.

Q: What's git cherry-pick?
Applies one specific commit from another branch onto your current branch, without merging everything. Example: a hotfix was committed on develop, but production needs just that one fix — you cherry-pick it onto hotfix/prod without pulling in unrelated develop changes.

Q: How do you find which commit introduced a bug?

git bisect start
git bisect bad                # current commit is broken
git bisect good <old-hash>    # this old commit was fine
